<BaseLayout title="About — Xiaoyue Gao">
  <PageIntro eyebrow="About" title="Training across computation and experiment" introduction="I am a cancer researcher trained in bioinformatics and experimental biology, now building toward computational biology with a practical understanding of how biological evidence is generated and validated." />

  <section class="content-grid shell">
    <p class="side-label">My path</p>
    <div class="stack">
      <article class="record"><div class="record-head"><div><h2>Biological science</h2><p>University of Chinese Academy of Sciences</p></div><p class="meta">2015–2019</p></div><p>I built a foundation in molecular and cellular biology and completed an international exchange at the Australian National University in 2018.</p></article>
      <article class="record"><div class="record-head"><div><h2>Bioinformatics</h2><p>Beijing Institute of Genomics, Chinese Academy of Sciences</p></div><p class="meta">2019–2022</p></div><p>My master’s research focused on cancer genomics. I performed the primary scATAC-seq analyses for a study of medulloblastoma subgroups and regulatory networks, and explored public scRNA-seq datasets in Seurat for independent evidence.</p></article>
      <article class="record"><div class="record-head"><div><h2>Experimental cancer biology</h2><p>University of Wisconsin–Madison</p></div><p class="meta">2022–Present</p></div><p>I joined the Cellular and Molecular Pathology PhD program to gain direct experience in experimental validation. My research examines DNA methylation and DNMT3B in B-cell lymphoma through genomic analysis, functional assays, cell models, and collaborative mouse studies.</p></article>
      <article class="record"><div class="record-head"><div><h2>Computational transition</h2><p>Current chapter</p></div><p class="meta">Developing</p></div><p>I am intentionally strengthening the computational side of my work. My long-term goal is to focus on data-driven biological questions while remaining closely connected to experimental design and validation.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-heading"><h2>How I work</h2><p>These principles come from specific research decisions rather than a list of abstract strengths.</p></div>
      <div class="card-grid">
        <article class="card"><p class="card-number">01</p><h3>Revisit assumptions</h3><p>When an inherited interpretation does not fully explain the data, I return to the underlying evidence and change direction when the results require it.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><p class="card-number">02</p><h3>Reframe the question</h3><p>When available data cannot answer the original question, I look for a meaningful question that the evidence can support.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><p class="card-number">03</p><h3>Connect analysis and validation</h3><p>I do not treat software output as a biological conclusion. I look for independent evidence and experiments that can test the interpretation.</p></article>
      </div>
      <p class="note">Most meaningful research is collaborative. I distinguish between work I performed, guidance I received, and contributions made by collaborators.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

